{% comment %}
  SHEmakes — browse.

  Filters replace the mega-menu. The island narrows the products Liquid already
  rendered, so ticking a filter is instant.

  Sorting and paging stay with Shopify on purpose: those need the whole
  catalogue, not the page of it in the browser, and doing them client-side
  would quietly show the wrong results.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  comment
    Shopify's own filters, when the shop has set any up (Search & Discovery).
    They are the only ones that can be honest about totals: they narrow the
    whole collection server-side, so the count and the pager are the filtered
    ones. Without them the island narrows the page in the browser, which is
    fast but only ever knows about the products on it.
  endcomment
  comment
    Server mode: Shopify's filters are on the page, or a tag filter is active.

    The second half matters because a filter that matches nothing leaves
    `collection.filters` empty — Shopify builds them from the products it
    found — and the page then fell back to the browser-side sidebar, which
    listed categories that no longer exist and offered a "Clear filters"
    button that could not clear a URL.
  endcomment
  assign has_filters = false
  if collection.filters.size > 0 or current_tags.size > 0
    assign has_filters = true
  endif

  comment
    The sidebar is on, minus the Surface group: that one was a single tick box
    on most collections, and it is what the shop asked to be rid of. The
    categories stay, and a collection with no category tags gets no sidebar at
    all rather than a "Filter" heading over nothing.
  endcomment
  unless section.settings.show_filters
    assign has_filters = false
  endunless

  comment
    Some collections are a single drop rather than a catalogue — New Arrivals
    is the shop's example — and a filter column beside one of those is a
    control that narrows a page nobody came to narrow. Named by handle,
    because the sidebar setting belongs to the template and every collection
    shares that template.
  endcomment
  assign bare = section.settings.no_filters | default: '' | downcase | split: ','
  for bare_handle in bare
    assign bare_clean = bare_handle | strip
    if bare_clean != blank and collection.handle == bare_clean
      assign has_filters = false
    endif
  endfor

  comment
    The strip above the pager belongs to one or two collections, not to the
    whole catalogue — it was offering a gang sheet builder on a page of blank
    ornaments. Named by handle for the same reason the filter list is: every
    collection without a template of its own shares this one.
  endcomment
  assign prompt_heading = section.settings.prompt_heading
  assign prompt_only = section.settings.prompt_handles | default: '' | downcase | split: ','
  if prompt_only.size > 0
    assign prompt_heading = ''
    for prompt_handle in prompt_only
      comment
        A loop variable cannot be reassigned in Liquid — `assign` to its name
        is ignored — so the stripped copy needs a name of its own. Without
        it every handle after the first kept its leading space and never
        matched.
      endcomment
      assign prompt_clean = prompt_handle | strip
      if prompt_clean != blank and collection.handle == prompt_clean
        assign prompt_heading = section.settings.prompt_heading
      endif
    endfor
  endif
-%}

{%- paginate collection.products by section.settings.per_page -%}
{%- assign products = '' -%}
{%- for product in collection.products -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    One snippet builds the card's row, for here and for the homepage strip —
    they draw the same card, so they have to send the same shape.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- capture row %}{% render 'sm-product-json', product: product, new_for_days: section.settings.new_for_days %}{% endcapture -%}
  {%- if forloop.first -%}
    {%- assign products = row -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- assign products = products | append: ',' | append: row -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}

{%- liquid
  assign filters = ''
  for block in section.blocks
    assign values = block.settings.values | split: ','
    assign cleaned = ''
    for value in values
      assign trimmed = value | strip | json
      if forloop.first
        assign cleaned = trimmed
      else
        assign cleaned = cleaned | append: ',' | append: trimmed
      endif
    endfor

    assign l = block.settings.label | json
    assign row = '{"label":' | append: l | append: ',"values":[' | append: cleaned | append: ']}'
    if forloop.first
      assign filters = row
    else
      assign filters = filters | append: ',' | append: row
    endif
  endfor
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The page opens on a band: breadcrumb, title, and the collection's own line
  under it — the template's shop header.

  Every photograph here is the shop's own, and it is a product — the first
  design in the collection, its own artwork on its own garment. The
  collection's admin image was tried first and is not a photograph: it is a
  marketing banner with its own headline set into it, which behind a page
  heading reads as two titles fighting. Behind the products sit prints from
  the shop floor bundled with the theme, matched on the handle, for a
  collection that is empty or filtered down to nothing. Stock photography was
  tried in this spot too and taken out: it is somebody else's work standing in
  for this shop's.

  A handle is stable in a way a title is not, and a collection whose handle
  says nothing still lands on a real print rather than on nothing. The picker
  overrides all of it.

  The last word of the title goes in the script face. `sm-heading` marks its
  accent with asterisks, and a collection title comes from the admin with none
  in it, so the marks are put in here — the title itself is untouched.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign head_image = section.settings.head_image

  comment
    The first design in the collection. A loop rather than `products.first`,
    because a collection filtered down to nothing has no first product and
    the fallback has to take over cleanly.
  endcomment
  unless head_image
    for head_product in collection.products limit: 1
      assign head_image = head_product.featured_image
    endfor
  endunless

  comment
    UV is tested before custom: `custom-uv-dtf` contains both words, and it is
    the UV that decides the picture.
  endcomment
  assign handle = collection.handle | downcase
  if handle contains 'uv' or handle contains 'decal' or handle contains 'cup' or handle contains 'wrap'
    assign head_photo = 'sm-cta-2.jpg'
  elsif handle contains 'new' or handle contains 'arrival'
    assign head_photo = 'sm-band-1.jpg'
  elsif handle contains 'blank' or handle contains 'apparel' or handle contains 'dtf' or handle contains 'transfer' or handle contains 'custom'
    assign head_photo = 'sm-band-2.jpg'
  else
    assign head_photo = 'sm-about.jpg'
  endif

  assign head_fallback = head_photo | asset_url

  assign title_words = collection.title | split: ' '
  assign last_word = title_words | last
  if title_words.size > 1
    assign title_start = collection.title | remove_last: last_word
    assign marked_title = title_start | append: '*' | append: last_word | append: '*'
  else
    comment
      A one-word collection — "Blanks" — has no last word to set apart, so the
      word itself takes the script face. Every collection then opens the same
      way instead of half of them opening plain.
    endcomment
    assign marked_title = '*' | append: collection.title | append: '*'
  endif
-%}
<div class="sm-pagehead sm-pagehead--photo">
  <div class="sm-pagehead__media" aria-hidden="true">
    {%- if head_image != blank -%}
      <img
        src="{{ head_image | image_url: width: 2000 }}"
        alt=""
        width="{{ head_image.width }}"
        height="{{ head_image.height }}"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      >
    {%- else -%}
      <img src="{{ head_fallback }}" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high">
    {%- endif -%}
    <span class="sm-pagehead__scrim"></span>
  </div>

  <div class="sm-container">
    <nav class="sm-crumbs sm-crumbs--center" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="{{ routes.root_url }}">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <span aria-current="page">{{ collection.title }}</span>
    </nav>

    {% render 'sm-heading', text: marked_title, tag: 'h1', class: 'sm-heading sm-heading--lg' %}

    {%- comment -%}
      Straight to the directions at the foot of the page, for anyone who has
      landed here not knowing how to order. `#sm-how` is the steps section.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if section.settings.how_label != blank -%}
      <p class="sm-pagehead__actions">
        <a class="sm-button sm-button--secondary" href="#sm-how">{{ section.settings.how_label }}</a>
      </p>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The description is off by default. The shop writes ordering instructions
      into it — "how to submit customs", the list of builder listings — which
      the option cards and the steps below now say properly, and printing both
      meant every collection opened with the same wall of centred bullets.
      The text is untouched in the admin; this only decides whether the page
      repeats it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if section.settings.show_description and collection.description != blank -%}
      <div class="sm-pagehead__intro sm-prose rte">{{ collection.description }}</div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>

<div class="sm-section sm-section--tight" data-sm-collection>
  <div class="sm-container">
    <div class="sm-browse__head">
      <div>
        <p class="sm-browse__count" id="sm-count">
          {%- liquid
            comment
              With a filter on, products_count is the filtered total across the
              whole collection — not the page — so the second number answers
              "how many match", which is what it was being read as anyway.
            endcomment
            assign filtered = current_tags.size
            for filter in collection.filters
              assign filtered = filtered | plus: filter.active_values.size
            endfor
          -%}

          {%- if filtered > 0 and collection.products_count == 0 -%}
            No designs match those filters
          {%- elsif filtered > 0 -%}
            Showing {{ collection.products.size }} of {{ collection.products_count }} designs that match
          {%- else -%}
            Showing {{ collection.products.size }} of {{ collection.products_count }} designs
          {%- endif -%}
        </p>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        Sorting needs the whole catalogue, so it stays with Shopify: a GET form
        that reloads with ?sort_by=. The island only narrows what is already on
        the page.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if collection.sort_options.size > 0 -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign sort_options = ''
          for option in collection.sort_options
            assign label = option.name | json
            assign value = option.value | json
            assign row = '{"label":' | append: label | append: ',"value":' | append: value | append: '}'
            if forloop.first
              assign sort_options = row
            else
              assign sort_options = sort_options | append: ',' | append: row
            endif
          endfor

          assign current_sort = collection.sort_by | default: collection.default_sort_by
        -%}

        {%- capture sort_props -%}
{ "label": "Sort by", "prefix": "Sort: ", "param": "sort_by", "value": {{ current_sort | json }}, "options": [{{ sort_options }}] }
        {%- endcapture -%}

        {%- capture sort_fallback -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The native control, for no-JS. React replaces it — a `<select>`
            draws its own popup and no stylesheet reaches inside it.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <form method="get" class="sm-browse__sort">
            <label class="visually-hidden" for="sm-sort">Sort by</label>
            <select id="sm-sort" name="sort_by" onchange="this.form.submit()">
              {%- for option in collection.sort_options -%}
                <option value="{{ option.value }}" {% if option.value == current_sort %}selected{% endif %}>
                  Sort: {{ option.name }}
                </option>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </select>
            <noscript><button type="submit" class="sm-button sm-button--secondary">Sort</button></noscript>
          </form>
        {%- endcapture -%}

        <div class="sm-browse__sort">
          {% render 'sm-island', name: 'select', props: sort_props, fallback: sort_fallback %}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      The groups are built before the sidebar is drawn: a collection whose tags
      match none of them would otherwise get a "Filter" heading over nothing,
      which is what the shop asked to be rid of.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- capture filter_groups -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Shopify hands back the tag as it is written on the product ("UV DTF"),
          and the block values are typed by hand, so the two are compared as
          handles — case and spacing stopped the active state from ever
          matching.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- capture current_handles -%}
          {%- for tag in current_tags -%}|{{ tag | handleize }}{%- endfor -%}|
        {%- endcapture -%}
        {%- assign current_handles = current_handles | strip -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          Which of the shop's categories exist as tags on this collection.

          A value that is not a real tag is not a filter: Shopify ignores the
          unknown handle and serves the whole collection, so "Puff" and
          "Blanks" looked live and quietly did nothing — 2,396 designs either
          way. Anything with no tag behind it is dropped rather than shown.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- capture known_handles -%}
          {%- for tag in collection.all_tags -%}|{{ tag | handleize }}{%- endfor -%}|
        {%- endcapture -%}
        {%- assign known_handles = known_handles | strip -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          The path Shopify reads tags from: /collections/all/tag1+tag2. Adding
          keeps what is already on, so two categories narrow each other the way
          the boxes suggest they do.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- capture current_path -%}
          {%- for tag in current_tags -%}{% unless forloop.first %}+{% endunless %}{{ tag | handleize }}{%- endfor -%}
        {%- endcapture -%}
        {%- assign current_path = current_path | strip -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          A collection can carry its own short list in place of the shared
          one. "Ornament & Santa Tray UV DTF" is two kinds of thing, and the
          thirty Christmas themes of the shared list were noise on it. Each
          line in the setting is `handle: Label=tag, Label=tag`; the first
          filter group takes those values on that collection only.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign own_values = '' -%}
        {%- assign own_lines = section.settings.filter_overrides | default: '' | newline_to_br | split: '<br />' -%}
        {%- for own_line in own_lines -%}
          {%- assign own_parts = own_line | split: ':' -%}
          {%- assign own_handle = own_parts[0] | strip -%}
          {%- if own_parts.size > 1 and own_handle == collection.handle -%}
            {%- assign own_values = own_line | remove_first: own_parts[0] | remove_first: ':' | strip -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}

        {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
          {%- assign values = block.settings.values | split: ',' -%}
          {%- if forloop.first and own_values != blank -%}
            {%- assign values = own_values | split: ',' -%}
          {%- endif -%}
          {%- if values.size > 0 -%}
            {%- capture options -%}
              {%- for value in values -%}
                {%- liquid
                  assign value_parts = value | split: '='
                  assign tag = value_parts | last | strip
                  assign tag_label = value_parts | first | strip
                  assign tag_handle = tag | handleize
                  assign needle = tag_handle | prepend: '|' | append: '|'

                  assign exists = false
                  if known_handles contains needle
                    assign exists = true
                  endif

                  assign is_on = false
                  if current_handles contains needle
                    assign is_on = true
                  endif
                -%}

                {%- if exists -%}
                  {%- liquid
                    comment
                      One theme at a time, as the shop asked. On: the link
                      clears it. Off: it replaces whatever was chosen before
                      — stacking tags narrowed the page to designs carrying
                      every one of them, which was usually nothing.
                    endcomment
                    if is_on
                      assign target = ''
                    else
                      assign target = tag_handle
                    endif
                  -%}

                  <a
                    class="sm-browse__option sm-browse__option--one{% if is_on %} is-on{% endif %}"
                    href="{{ collection.url }}{% if target != '' %}/{{ target }}{% endif %}#sm-results"
                  >
                    <span class="sm-browse__box" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    {{ tag_label }}
                  </a>
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
            {%- endcapture -%}

            {%- comment -%} A group whose every value was dropped is not a group. {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if options != blank -%}
              <details class="sm-browse__group" open>
                <summary class="sm-eyebrow">{{ block.settings.label }}</summary>
                <div class="sm-browse__group-body">{{ options }}</div>
              </details>
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          Shopify's own filters, minus the two the shop asked to drop:
          availability (nearly everything is in stock, so it never narrowed
          anything) and the price range.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- for filter in collection.filters -%}
          {%- assign filter_handle = filter.label | handleize -%}
          {%- if filter.type == 'price_range' or filter_handle contains 'availability' or filter_handle contains 'in-stock' -%}
            {%- continue -%}
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- case filter.type -%}
            {%- when 'list' -%}
              <details class="sm-browse__group" open>
                <summary class="sm-eyebrow">{{ filter.label }}</summary>
                <div class="sm-browse__group-body">

                {%- comment -%}
                  One theme at a time, which is how the shop wants it read.

                  Shopify's own `url_to_add` stacks a value onto whatever is
                  already chosen, so picking four themes narrowed the page to
                  the designs carrying all four — usually none. These links
                  start from the bare collection instead, so choosing a theme
                  replaces the one before it. Sorting survives, because that
                  is not a filter.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- liquid
                  assign base_url = collection.url
                  assign joiner = '?'
                  if collection.sort_by != blank
                    assign base_url = base_url | append: '?sort_by=' | append: collection.sort_by
                    assign joiner = '&'
                  endif
                -%}

                {%- for value in filter.values -%}
                  {%- liquid
                    assign option_value = value.value | url_encode
                    if value.active
                      assign option_url = base_url
                    else
                      assign option_url = base_url | append: joiner | append: value.param_name | append: '=' | append: option_value
                    endif
                  -%}
                  <a
                    class="sm-browse__option sm-browse__option--one{% if value.active %} is-on{% endif %}"
                    href="{{ option_url }}#sm-results"
                    {% if value.count == 0 and value.active == false %}aria-disabled="true"{% endif %}
                  >
                    <span class="sm-browse__box" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    {{ value.label }}
                    <span class="sm-browse__tally">{{ value.count }}</span>
                  </a>
                {%- endfor -%}
                </div>
              </details>

            {%- when 'price_range' -%}
              <details class="sm-browse__group" open>
                <summary class="sm-eyebrow">{{ filter.label }}</summary>

                <form method="get" action="{{ collection.url }}#sm-results" class="sm-browse__range">
                  {%- for other in collection.filters -%}
                    {%- unless other.type == 'price_range' -%}
                      {%- for value in other.active_values -%}
                        <input type="hidden" name="{{ value.param_name }}" value="{{ value.value }}">
                      {%- endfor -%}
                    {%- endunless -%}
                  {%- endfor -%}

                  <label class="visually-hidden" for="sm-price-min">Lowest price</label>
                  <input id="sm-price-min" type="number" inputmode="numeric" min="0" placeholder="Min"
                         name="{{ filter.min_value.param_name }}" value="{{ filter.min_value.value }}">

                  <span aria-hidden="true">–</span>

                  <label class="visually-hidden" for="sm-price-max">Highest price</label>
                  <input id="sm-price-max" type="number" inputmode="numeric" min="0" placeholder="Max"
                         name="{{ filter.max_value.param_name }}" value="{{ filter.max_value.value }}">

                  <button type="submit" class="sm-button sm-button--secondary">Apply</button>
                </form>
              </details>
          {%- endcase -%}
        {%- endfor -%}

    {%- endcapture -%}
    {%- assign filter_groups = filter_groups | strip -%}
    {%- if filter_groups == blank -%}
      {%- assign has_filters = false -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if has_filters -%}
      <div class="sm-browse sm-browse--server">
      {%- comment -%}
        Plain links, not a form: each one is a URL Shopify builds, so the page
        that comes back is filtered, counted and paged by Shopify. The #results
        fragment is what puts the shopper back at the grid rather than at the
        top of the page — the answer to "it should scroll up".
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <aside class="sm-browse__filters sm-browse__filters--real" id="sm-filters">
        <p class="sm-browse__filters-title">Filter</p>

        {%- comment -%}
          The shop's own categories, as Shopify tag links.

          These went missing when the sidebar moved to Shopify's filters:
          Search & Discovery is set up with Availability and Price only, and
          the theme's Type/tag groups could only ever narrow the page in the
          browser. `/collections/<handle>/<tag>` is Shopify's built-in tag
          route, so these narrow the whole collection and the count and the
          pager are the filtered ones — no app configuration needed.

          One at a time: a second tag would need the +tag1+tag2 form, and the
          shop has never had more than one category on at once.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {{ filter_groups }}

        {%- liquid
          comment
            Liquid takes no filters inside an `if`, so the tally is built first.
          endcomment
          assign active_count = 0
          for filter in collection.filters
            assign active_count = active_count | plus: filter.active_values.size
          endfor
        -%}

        {%- if current_tags.size > 0 -%}
          {%- assign active_count = active_count | plus: current_tags.size -%}
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- if active_count > 0 -%}
          <a class="sm-browse__clear" href="{{ collection.url }}#sm-results">Clear all</a>
        {%- endif -%}
      </aside>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- capture props -%}
{
  "products": [{{ products }}],
  {%- comment -%}
    Never any groups: the island draws a sidebar of its own when it is given
    some, and it could only narrow the page in the browser while the count and
    the pager spoke for the whole collection. Filtering is the Liquid sidebar
    above, on Shopify's own tag routes.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  "filters": [],
  "currency": {{ cart.currency.iso_code | json }},
  "prompt": {
    "heading": {{ prompt_heading | json }},
    "body": {{ section.settings.prompt_body | json }},
    "label": {{ section.settings.prompt_label | json }},
    "url": {{ section.settings.prompt_url | default: '#' | json }}
  }
}
    {%- endcapture -%}

    {%- capture fallback -%}
      <div class="sm-browse__grid">
        {%- for product in collection.products -%}
          <a class="sm-card" href="{{ product.url }}">
            <div class="sm-card__media">
              {%- if product.featured_image -%}
                <img src="{{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 600 }}" alt="{{ product.title | escape }}" loading="lazy">
              {%- endif -%}
            </div>
            <div class="sm-card__body">
              <h3 class="sm-card__title">{{ product.title }}</h3>
              <p class="sm-card__price">{{ product.price | money }}</p>
            </div>
          </a>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    {%- endcapture -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The anchor wraps the grid rather than being an empty div before it: an
      empty element collapses to zero height and reported its position as the
      top of the page, so the filter links landed the shopper back at the
      masthead.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div id="sm-results">
      {%- if has_filters and collection.products.size == 0 -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Nothing matched. The way out is a link that drops the filters from
          the URL — the island's button only ever cleared its own state.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="sm-browse__empty">
          <p>No designs match those filters.</p>
          <a class="sm-button sm-button--secondary" href="{{ collection.url }}#sm-results">Clear filters</a>
        </div>
      {%- else -%}
        {% render 'sm-island', name: 'collection-grid', props: props, fallback: fallback %}
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    {%- if has_filters -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%} Paging stays on Shopify. {%- endcomment -%}
    <div id="sm-pager">
      {% render 'sm-pagination', paginate: paginate, label: 'designs' %}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

{%- endpaginate -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "SHEmakes collection",
  "tag": "section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "head_image",
      "label": "Header photo",
      "info": "Replaces the photograph behind the title on every collection page. Without it, the first design in the collection is used."
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "filter_overrides",
      "label": "A collection's own filter",
      "info": "One line per collection: handle: Label=tag, Label=tag. That collection's first filter shows only these."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "no_filters",
      "label": "Collections with no filters",
      "info": "Comma separated handles. These open as one plain grid — no sidebar, no filter links.",
      "default": "newly-added"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_filters",
      "label": "Filter sidebar",
